.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt READAHEAD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm readahead ,
.Nm posix_fadvise
.Nd advise the kernel of reads to come
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn readahead "int fd" "off_t offset" "size_t count"
.Ft int
.Fn posix_fadvise "int fd" "off_t offset" "off_t len" "int advice"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn readahead
starts the reads of the pages of the
.Fa count
bytes at
.Fa offset
of
.Fa fd ,
the rest of the file for a
.Fa count
of 0, and returns 0 without waiting for them, as
.Fn posix_fadvise
with
.Dv POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED
does.
.Pp
.Fn posix_fadvise
takes one
.Fa advice
for the
.Fa len
bytes at
.Fa offset ,
the rest of the file for a
.Fa len
of 0:
.Bl -tag -width "POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL"
.It Dv POSIX_FADV_NORMAL
Reads of the descriptor are read ahead in windows of at most 128 KiB.
.It Dv POSIX_FADV_RANDOM
A read that misses the page cache reads the pages it asks for and no
more.
.It Dv POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL
Windows of at most 256 KiB, and no
.Dv POSIX_FADV_RANDOM .
.It Dv POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED
The range's pages are read into the page cache without waiting, at most
the descriptor's window or what one request to the disk moves, whichever
is larger.
.It Dv POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED
The page cache drops the range's whole pages, and a last partial page
that ends the file.
.It Dv POSIX_FADV_NOREUSE
Nothing changes.
.El
.Pp
Readahead is Linux's on-demand readahead.
A read that misses the cache reads a window from the page it wants: at
the start of the file, or going on from where the descriptor's last read
ended, four, two or one times the pages it wants, rounded up to a power
of two, up to the most a window takes; otherwise, when the cache holds
more pages just before it than the read wants, as many pages as those and
the read's together, counting those twice when they reach the file's
start, up to the most; and else only the pages the read wants.
A page near the window's end is marked, and a read that reaches it starts
the next window, two to four times larger up to the most, which a kernel
thread,
.Sy kreadahead ,
reads while the reader goes on; a read that reaches a page such a thread
is reading waits for that read.
Only the ext2 file system's regular files have a page cache that reads
ahead; for any other file the advice is checked and returns 0.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 225 for
.Fn readahead ,
250 for
.Fn fadvise64 ,
which takes a 32-bit length, and 272 for
.Fn fadvise64_64 ,
which takes a 64-bit length and is the one musl calls for
.Fn posix_fadvise .
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm readahead
is call 187 and
.Nm fadvise64
221, with the 64-bit offset and length in RSI and RDX and the advice in
R10; there is no
.Nm fadvise64_64 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0, or for
.Fn readahead
-1 with
.Va errno
set; musl's
.Fn posix_fadvise
returns the error number itself.
.Sh EXAMPLES
GNU
.Xr cp 1 ,
.Xr cat 1
and
.Xr sort 1
call
.Fn posix_fadvise
on what they read, and BusyBox's
.Xr readahead 1
calls
.Fn readahead
on each file it names.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ESPIPE]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open, was opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
or for
.Fn readahead
is not open for reading.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
For
.Fn readahead ,
.Fa fd
is not a regular file; for
.Fn posix_fadvise ,
.Fa len
is negative or
.Fa advice
is none of the six.
.It Bq Er ESPIPE
For
.Fn posix_fadvise ,
.Fa fd
is a pipe or a FIFO.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr read 2
